## VIII. Operationalizing Kim's "Resonance Memory"

### Kim's Proposal

Store only high-resonance episodes:

<pre class="overflow-x-auto"><code>if R &gt; threshold:
    save_to_memory(episode)</code></pre>

### RSVP Translation: Selective Consolidation

Define **memory trace strength**:

$$\boxed{M(t, t') = \Omega(t') \cdot e^{-\frac{(t-t')^2}{2\tau_{\text{mem}}^2}} \cdot \left[1 + \frac{J(t')}{\langle J \rangle}\right]}$$

Memories decay exponentially but are weighted by:
1. **Resonance at encoding** ($\Omega(t')$)
2. **Jouissance rate** (high-meaning moments persist longer)
3. **Time since encoding** (Gaussian forgetting)

**Storage criterion:**

$$\text{Store episode } t' \text{ if } M(t, t') &gt; M_{\text{threshold}}$$

**Retrieval dynamics:**

When planning action at time $t$, retrieve top-$k$ memories by $M(t, t')$. Use them to modulate current $\Phi_a(t)$:

$$\Phi_a^{\text{retrieved}}(t) = \Phi_a(t) + \sum_{t' \in \text{top-}k} M(t, t') \cdot \Phi_a(t')$$

This implements **resonance-weighted episodic memory** ‚Äî exactly Kim's architecture, now with explicit decay dynamics.

