</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{abstract}
Juyoung Kim‚Äôs ``Emotion as Rhythmic Inference'' (ERI) treats emotion as a dynamic, rhythm‚Äêlike phenomenon arising from gaps, phase differences, and resonance in predictive processing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{kim2025emotion}. In parallel, the Relativistic Scalar--Vector Plenum (RSVP) models cognition as non‚Äêequilibrium dynamics on a triadic manifold of scalar potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, vector flow </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and entropy </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, grounded in the free‚Äêenergy principle </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{friston2010free, friston2015active}. We develop a unified formalism that maps ERI‚Äôs phenomenology onto RSVP‚Äôs field geometry and thermodynamics, yielding a differential law of rhythmic inference in which consciousness grows under moderate uncertainty, active gradients, and productive phase offset, and decays under friction and curvature flattening. We derive testable predictions for phase‚Äêlocking, temporal depth, and resonance memory, and articulate ethical constraints for human‚ÄìAI coupling consistent with embodied and information‚Äêgeometric perspectives </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{friston2022synchrony, hohwy2013predictive, varela1991embodied}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{abstract}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tableofcontents</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Part I: Phenomenology of Rhythmic Inference}
Kim‚Äôs ERI frames emotion as a continuous process of inference rather than a set of discrete states: systems oscillate between misalignment and re‚Äêalignment of internal predictions with external signals, and this rhythmic management of imperfection sustains learning and experience </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{kim2025emotion}. The ERI triad comprises </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{gap} (an unresolved predictive difference), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{phase alignment} (temporally structured misalignment/resynchrony), and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{resonance} (sustained coupling under bounded distance). This view is consonant with predictive processing and active inference, which formalize perception and action as the minimization of variational free energy </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{friston2010free, friston2015active, hohwy2013predictive}.

Neuroscientifically, oscillations, (de)synchronization, and cross‚Äêfrequency coupling are central to cognition and memory, supporting ERI‚Äôs emphasis on phase dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{buzsaki2019brain}. Phenomenologically, ERI‚Äôs ``time‚Äêgiving''---the expansion of subjective duration during deep, resonant dialogue---aligns with critical slowing near low‚Äêcurvature regions of the information‚Äìgeometric manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{yeung2023critical, friston2022synchrony}. Affect, on this view, is the felt signature of curvature modulation in predictive precision.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Part II: Mathematical Development on the RSVP Manifold}
We adopt the RSVP triad </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> v,S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>: a scalar potential encoding symbolic prediction, a vector field encoding affective drive, and an entropy density encoding uncertainty. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> v,S]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote a free‚Äêenergy functional capturing prediction error, complexity, and energetic terms </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{friston2010free, pezzulo2020modelling, ramstead2022active}. Variational dynamics induce coupled evolution:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{align}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>= -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> v + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span> S + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>R, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:phi}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> v </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>= -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> v) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>) - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> v, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:v}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t S </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>= D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 S + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> v) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>f(U)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>R(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:S}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{align}
where the last terms couple uncertainty </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, gap magnitude </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and a phase factor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> that captures rhythmic alignment (cf.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>ERI). The solenoidal component in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eqref</span></span><span>{eq:v} corresponds to conservative circulation around iso‚Äêfree‚Äêenergy contours, while dissipative terms drive descent, a decomposition fundamental to active inference </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{friston2022synchrony}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Information geometry and curvature.}
Curvature of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (e.g., the Hessian </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) provides an information‚Äìgeometric account of precision and stiffness of inference </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{ramstead2022active}. Near small </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Tr}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 F)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, perturbations decay slowly (critical slowing), underpinning ERI‚Äôs ``time‚Äêgiving'' phenomenon </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{yeung2023critical}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{A consciousness growth law.}
